1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:07,150
Chapter 25: Obstetrical Analgesia and Anesthesia.

3
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:17,950
General Principles Analgesia And Sedation During Labor Nerve Blocks Neuraxial Analgesia SPINAL (SUBARACHNOID) BLOCK Epidural Analgesia.

4
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:20,062
General Anesthesia.

5
00:00:20,062 --> 00:00:21,825
References.

6
00:00:21,825 --> 00:00:25,737
Obstetrical anesthesia presents unique challenges.

7
00:00:25,737 --> 00:00:31,800
Labor begins without warning, and anesthesia may be required within minutes of a full meal.

8
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:36,350
Vomiting with aspiration of gastric contents is a constant threat.

9
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:47,300
The usual physiological adaptations of pregnancy require special consideration, especially with disorders such as preeclampsia, placental abruption, or sepsis.

10
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:53,837
Obstetrical analgesia and anesthesia have become extraordinarily safe over the past 40 years .

11
00:00:53,837 --> 00:01:10,137
Anesthetic-related maternal mortality rates decreased more than 60 percent during this time, and from 2007 to 2017, only 0.4 percent of 6765 maternal deaths were due to anesthesia complications .

12
00:01:10,137 --> 00:01:20,150
Creanga and colleagues (2017) reported the contribution of anesthetics to pregnancy-associated deaths has declined markedly in the United States .

13
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:29,537
Approximately two thirds of deaths associated with general anesthesia were caused by induction problems or intubation failures during cesarean delivery.

14
00:01:29,537 --> 00:01:43,675
Deaths associated with regional analgesia were caused by spinal or epidural blocks reaching higher than planned spinal levels—26 percent; respiratory failure—19 percent; and drug reaction—19 percent.

15
00:01:43,675 --> 00:01:46,225
Figure 25-1.

16
00:01:46,225 --> 00:01:55,150
Contribution of anesthetic complications to pregnancy-associated deaths—United States, 1987–2013.

17
00:01:55,150 --> 00:02:04,987
Of factors contributing to improved obstetrical anesthesia safety, the increased use of regional analgesia for labor and delivery is the most significant.

18
00:02:04,987 --> 00:02:18,150
For general anesthesia, the improved case-fatality rate is especially notable considering that this method is now used for the highestrisk patients and for emergencies with decision-incision intervals <15 minutes .

19
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:26,500
In addition, the incidence of aspiration, hypoxia, or other respiratory events has declined during the past three decades.

20
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:38,662
This is likely due to use of difficult-airway algorithms, advanced airway equipment, and increased in-house anesthesia staffing (Davies, 2017; Lim, 2018).

21
00:02:38,662 --> 00:02:47,237
Despite these encouraging results with general anesthesia, rising complications with regional analgesia techniques are now reported .

22
00:02:47,237 --> 00:02:49,300
General Principles.

23
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,862
# Obstetrical Anesthesia Services.

24
00:02:52,862 --> 00:02:59,537
Every obstetrician should be proficient in local and pudendal analgesia for select circumstances.

25
00:02:59,537 --> 00:03:11,150
However, it is preferable for an anesthesiologist or anesthetist to provide rapid and reliable pain relief to allow the obstetrician to focus attention on the laboring woman and her fetus.

26
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:27,737
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) and the American Society of Anesthesiologists (2016) have reaffirmed prior directives that a woman’s request for labor pain relief is sufficient indication for its provision.

27
00:03:27,737 --> 00:03:36,975
Identification of any of the risk factors shown in Table 25-1 should prompt consultation and collaboration with anesthesia personnel.

28
00:03:36,975 --> 00:03:43,262
The resulting plan should ideally minimize the need for emergency anesthesia in this high-risk group.

29
00:03:43,262 --> 00:03:45,787
Table 25-1.

30
00:03:45,787 --> 00:04:03,200
Maternal Factors That May Prompt Anesthetic Consultation Goals for optimizing obstetrical anesthesia services have been established by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) and the American Society of Anesthesiologists (2016).

31
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:08,437
To achieve these goals, the following is a partial list of requisites: 1.

32
00:04:08,437 --> 00:04:19,637
Availability of a licensed practitioner credentialed to administer an appropriate anesthetic whenever necessary and to maintain support of vital functions in an obstetrical emergency 2.

33
00:04:19,637 --> 00:04:30,387
Availability of anesthesia personnel to permit the start of cesarean delivery within 30 minutes of the decision to perform the procedure (decision–incision time) 3.

34
00:04:30,387 --> 00:04:41,025
Anesthesia personnel immediately available to support an emergency cesarean delivery during the active labor of a woman attempting vaginal birth after prior cesarean 4.

35
00:04:41,025 --> 00:04:47,762
Appointment of a qualified anesthesiologist to be responsible for all anesthetics administered 5.

36
00:04:47,932 --> 00:04:57,682
Availability of a qualified physician with obstetrical privileges to perform operative vaginal or cesarean delivery during anesthesia administration 6.

37
00:04:57,632 --> 00:05:04,982
Availability of equipment, facilities, and support personnel equal to that provided in a surgical suite 7.

38
00:05:04,982 --> 00:05:13,519
Immediate availability of personnel, other than the surgical team, to assume responsibility for resuscitation of a depressed newborn .

39
00:05:13,519 --> 00:05:19,507
To meet these goals, 24-hour in-house anesthesia staffing is usually necessary.

40
00:05:19,507 --> 00:05:23,882
Providing such service in smaller facilities is more challenging.

41
00:05:23,882 --> 00:05:33,132
This is underscored by the fact that approximately 40 percent of all hospitals providing obstetrical care have fewer than 500 deliveries per year .

42
00:05:33,132 --> 00:05:42,344
Bell and coworkers (2000) calculated the financial burden that may be incurred to provide 24/7 obstetrical anesthesia coverage.

43
00:05:42,344 --> 00:05:53,844
They concluded that such coverage could not operate profitably at their tertiary referral institution, given the average indemnity and Medicaid reimbursement for labor epidural analgesia.

44
00:05:53,844 --> 00:06:09,644
Compounding this burden, some third-party payers have denied reimbursement for epidural analgesia in the absence of a specific medical indication—an approach repudiated by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b).

45
00:06:09,644 --> 00:06:12,594
# Principles of Pain Relief.

46
00:06:12,594 --> 00:06:23,182
Labor pain caused by uterine contractions and cervical dilation is transmitted through visceral afferent sympathetic nerves entering the spinal cord from T10 through L1.

47
00:06:23,182 --> 00:06:31,194
Later, perineal stretching transmits painful stimuli through the pudendal nerve and sacral nerves from S2 through S4.

48
00:06:31,194 --> 00:06:37,632
Cortical responses to pain and anxiety are complex and may be influenced by multiple factors.

49
00:06:37,632 --> 00:06:44,469
Labor pain is a highly individual response to variable stimuli that are uniquely received and interpreted .

50
00:06:44,469 --> 00:06:52,269
These stimuli are modified by emotional, motivational, cognitive, social, and cultural circumstances.

51
00:06:52,269 --> 00:07:02,182
Specific modifiers are maternal expectations for her childbirth experience, age, antepartum labor education, and quality of emotional support.

52
00:07:02,182 --> 00:07:09,332
Major causes of dissatisfaction with pain relief are perceived labor pain and delay in ameliorating the pain .

53
00:07:09,332 --> 00:07:16,694
A mother’s physiological responses to labor pain may influence maternal and fetal well-being and labor progress.

54
00:07:16,694 --> 00:07:24,619
First, a higher metabolic rate leads to more oxygen consumption and the resultant hyperventilation may induce hypocarbia.

55
00:07:24,619 --> 00:07:31,719
Second, the sympathetic nervous system response to pain leads to elevated circulating catecholamine levels.

56
00:07:31,719 --> 00:07:38,319
These in turn raise cardiac output and vascular resistance, which may raise maternal blood pressure.

57
00:07:38,319 --> 00:07:44,894
Catecholamines also directly can adversely affect uterine activity and uteroplacental blood flow.

58
00:07:44,894 --> 00:07:52,119
Last, pain, stress, and anxiety cause release of stress hormones such as cortisol and endorphins.

59
00:07:52,119 --> 00:07:56,907
Effective analgesia attenuates or eliminates these responses.

60
00:07:56,907 --> 00:08:00,007
Analgesia And Sedation During Labor.

61
00:08:00,007 --> 00:08:10,594
If uterine contractions cause discomfort, pain relief with a narcotic such as meperidine, plus an antiemetic tranquilizer such as promethazine, is appropriate.

62
00:08:10,594 --> 00:08:21,482
With a successful program of analgesia and sedation, discomfort usually is felt at the acme of a uterine contraction, and the mother can rest quietly between contractions.

63
00:08:21,482 --> 00:08:29,994
Appropriate drug selection and administration from the medications shown in Table 25-2 safely accomplishes these objectives.

64
00:08:29,994 --> 00:08:32,544
Table 25-2.

65
00:08:32,544 --> 00:08:38,182
Typical Parenteral Analgesic Agents for Labor Pain # Parenteral Agents.

66
00:08:38,182 --> 00:08:50,844
Meperidine and Promethazine Meperidine, 50 to 100 mg, with promethazine, 25 mg, may be administered intramuscularly (IM) at intervals of 2 to 4 hours.

67
00:08:50,844 --> 00:08:56,344
Analgesia is maximal 30 to 45 minutes after an IM injection.

68
00:08:56,344 --> 00:09:06,357
An almost immediate effect is achieved by giving meperidine intravenously (IV) in doses of 25 to 50 mg every 1 to 2 hours.

69
00:09:06,357 --> 00:09:13,632
In an earlier study, meperidine was found to be the most common opioid used worldwide for pain relief in labor .

70
00:09:13,632 --> 00:09:22,069
Tsui and associates (2004) found meperidine to be superior to placebo for pain relief in the first stage of labor.

71
00:09:22,069 --> 00:09:30,932
In a randomized investigation at Parkland Hospital, IV patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) with meperidine was found to be an

72
00:09:31,084 --> 00:09:35,859
inexpensive and reasonably effective method for labor analgesia .

73
00:09:35,809 --> 00:09:43,021
Women received 50-mg meperidine with 25-mg promethazine IV as an initial bolus.

74
00:09:43,021 --> 00:09:51,109
Thereafter, an infusion pump was set to deliver 15 mg of meperidine every 10 minutes as needed until delivery.

75
00:09:51,109 --> 00:09:59,484
Neonatal sedation, as measured by need for naloxone treatment in the delivery room, was identified in 3 percent of newborns.

76
00:09:59,484 --> 00:10:07,484
Meperidine readily crosses the placenta, and its depressant effect in the fetus closely follows peak maternal analgesia.

77
00:10:07,484 --> 00:10:15,334
The active metabolite of meperidine, normeperidine, has a half-life of approximately 72 hours in the neonate.

78
00:10:15,334 --> 00:10:20,896
Therefore, other opioids discussed below are favored for labor analgesia.

79
00:10:20,896 --> 00:10:34,321
Butorphanol This synthetic opioid-receptor agonist-antagonist narcotic, given in 1- to 2mg IV doses every 4 hours as needed, compares favorably with meperidine for labor analgesia.

80
00:10:34,321 --> 00:10:39,109
Its major side effects are somnolence, dizziness, and dysphoria.

81
00:10:39,109 --> 00:10:43,359
Neonatal respiratory depression is less than with meperidine.

82
00:10:43,359 --> 00:10:51,334
Importantly, the two drugs are not given contiguously because butorphanol antagonizes the narcotic effects of meperidine.

83
00:10:51,334 --> 00:11:03,984
Hatjis and Meis (1986) described a transient sinusoidal fetal heart rate pattern following butorphanol administration, but no short-term maternal or neonatal adverse sequelae were noted .

84
00:11:03,984 --> 00:11:11,471
Fentanyl This short-acting and potent synthetic opioid may be given in doses of 50 to 100 µg IV every hour.

85
00:11:11,471 --> 00:11:18,909
Its main disadvantage is a short duration of action, which requires frequent dosing or use of a PCA pump.

86
00:11:18,909 --> 00:11:32,346
Atkinson and coworkers (1994) reported that butorphanol provided better initial analgesia than fentanyl and was associated with fewer requests for additional medication or for epidural analgesia.

87
00:11:32,346 --> 00:11:37,896
Remifentanil This ultrashort-acting opioid can be given using a PCA pump .

88
00:11:37,896 --> 00:11:45,096
Remifentanil is administered through a dedicated IV cannula to avoid bolus dosing, which can lead to apnea.

89
00:11:45,096 --> 00:11:54,521
Both respiratory monitoring and 1:1 nursing-to-patient ratio help watch for apneic spells, which occur in 25 percent of treated women .

90
00:11:54,521 --> 00:12:03,984
In the RemiPCA SAFE Network Study, moderate maternal hypoxia (SaO2 <94 percent) was documented in a fourth of women .

91
00:12:03,984 --> 00:12:14,921
A multicenter randomized study showed PCA-administered remifentanil halved the proportion of women subsequently requesting epidural analgesia compared with IM meperidine .

92
00:12:14,921 --> 00:12:19,546
Parenteral Agent Safety Parenteral sedation is not without risks.

93
00:12:19,546 --> 00:12:28,596
Hawkins and colleagues (1997) reported that 4 of 129 maternal anesthetic-related deaths were from such sedation.

94
00:12:28,596 --> 00:12:34,484
One was from aspiration, two from inadequate ventilation, and one from overdosage.

95
00:12:34,484 --> 00:12:39,196
Narcotics used during labor may cause newborn respiratory depression.

96
00:12:39,196 --> 00:12:46,659
Naloxone is a narcotic antagonist capable of reversing respiratory depression induced by opioid narcotics.

97
00:12:46,659 --> 00:12:52,434
It acts by displacing the narcotic from specific receptors in the central nervous system.

98
00:12:52,434 --> 00:12:59,846
After adequate ventilation has been established, naloxone may be given to a newborn whose mother received narcotics.

99
00:12:59,846 --> 00:13:07,259
Naloxone is contraindicated in a newborn of a narcotic-addicted mother because withdrawal symptoms may be precipitated .

100
00:13:07,259 --> 00:13:09,859
# Nitrous Oxide.

101
00:13:09,859 --> 00:13:19,346
A self-administered mixture of 50-percent nitrous oxide (N2O) and oxygen may provide satisfactory analgesia during labor .

102
00:13:19,346 --> 00:13:28,896
Some preparations are premixed in a single cylinder (Entonox), and in others, a blender mixes the two gases from separate tanks (Nitronox).

103
00:13:28,896 --> 00:13:35,059
The gases are connected to a breathing circuit through a valve that opens only when the patient inspires.

104
00:13:35,059 --> 00:13:47,446
The use of intermittent nitrous oxide did not alter the rate of epidural analgesia but did improve birth experience satisfaction rates (Bobb, 2016; Lim, 2018).

105
00:13:47,446 --> 00:13:53,059
Maternal side effects include nausea, vomiting, dizziness, and drowsiness.

106
00:13:53,059 --> 00:14:05,046
Neonatal Apgar scores or umbilical cord blood gas results did not differ in women using nitrous oxide compared with those selecting other pain management methods or no analgesia in labor .

107
00:14:05,046 --> 00:14:06,871
Nerve Blocks.

108
00:14:06,871 --> 00:14:11,784
Various nerve blocks provide pain relief during labor and/or delivery.

109
00:14:11,784 --> 00:14:18,971
These include pudendal, paracervical, and neuraxial blocks such as spinal, epidural, dural-puncture

110
00:14:19,132 --> 00:14:23,132
epidural, and combined spinal-epidural techniques.

111
00:14:23,082 --> 00:14:25,819
# Anesthetic Agents.

112
00:14:25,819 --> 00:14:31,682
Commonly used nerve block anesthetics are summarized in Table 25-3.

113
00:14:31,682 --> 00:14:38,644
The dose of each agent varies widely and is dependent on the particular nerve block and physical status of the woman.

114
00:14:38,644 --> 00:14:49,707
The onset, duration, and quality of analgesia can be enhanced by increasing the anesthetic agent’s dose, concentration, and volume or by altering its delivery mode.

115
00:14:49,707 --> 00:14:58,482
With their use, safety tenets include incremental boluses of the agent and carefully monitoring for early warning signs of adverse effects.

116
00:14:58,482 --> 00:15:04,319
Appropriate equipment and personnel to manage these reactions must be immediately available.

117
00:15:04,319 --> 00:15:06,932
Table 25-3.

118
00:15:06,932 --> 00:15:19,119
Commonly Used Local Anesthetic Agents in Obstetrics Local analgesic agents are manufactured in more than one concentration and ampule size, and this raises the potential for dosing errors.

119
00:15:19,119 --> 00:15:26,844
Most often, serious toxicity follows inadvertent intravenous, subarachnoid, or subdural injection.

120
00:15:26,844 --> 00:15:34,507
Systemic toxicity from local anesthetics typically manifests in the central nervous and cardiovascular systems.

121
00:15:34,507 --> 00:15:44,657
For this reason, before epidural analgesia is initiated, a mixture of a small amount of local anesthetic drug and epinephrine is given as a test dose.

122
00:15:44,657 --> 00:15:52,294
Epinephrine with its associated tachycardia is used as a marker to help identify incorrect placement if it occurs.

123
00:15:52,294 --> 00:16:01,869
A sudden significant elevation in the maternal heart rate or blood pressure within 1 to 2 minutes after administration suggests intravenous catheter placement.

124
00:16:01,869 --> 00:16:12,582
A block extending over wider than expected spinal levels and/or dense motor blockade indicate inadvertent catheter placement into the subarachnoid or into the subdural space.

125
00:16:12,582 --> 00:16:20,607
Central Nervous System Toxicity Early symptoms are those of stimulation, but as serum levels rise, depression follows.

126
00:16:20,607 --> 00:16:27,932
Symptoms may include light-headedness, dizziness, tinnitus, metallic taste, and numbness of the tongue and mouth.

127
00:16:27,932 --> 00:16:34,644
Patients may also show bizarre behavior, slurred speech, and muscle fasciculation and excitation.

128
00:16:34,644 --> 00:16:39,869
Ultimately, generalized convulsions and loss of consciousness could result.

129
00:16:39,869 --> 00:16:51,507
For neural and cardiac toxicity treatment, a rapid IV infusion of 20percent intralipids effectively disassociates anesthetic drugs from neural and myocardial cell membranes.

130
00:16:51,507 --> 00:16:58,982
For patients weighing >70 kg, a 100-mL bolus dose of lipid emulsion is given.

131
00:16:58,982 --> 00:17:05,144
Boluses can be repeated once or twice every 5 minutes for persistent cardiac instability.

132
00:17:05,144 --> 00:17:10,807
The initial bolus is followed by a 200-mL infusion over 15 minutes .

133
00:17:10,807 --> 00:17:15,669
For convulsions, an airway is established, and oxygen delivered.

134
00:17:15,669 --> 00:17:24,132
To halt seizures, lorazepam, 4 mg IV slowly, is administered and may be repeated once after 10 to 15 minutes.

135
00:17:24,132 --> 00:17:33,469
Alternatively, diazepam, 5 to 10 mg IV every 10 to 15 minutes up to a total dose of 30 mg, can be used.

136
00:17:33,469 --> 00:17:40,382
Succinylcholine abolishes the peripheral manifestations of the convulsions and allows tracheal intubation.

137
00:17:40,382 --> 00:17:47,194
Magnesium sulfate, administered according to the regimen for eclampsia, also controls convulsions .

138
00:17:47,194 --> 00:17:56,619
Abnormal fetal heart rate patterns such as late decelerations or persistent bradycardia may develop from maternal hypoxia and lactic acidosis.

139
00:17:56,619 --> 00:18:07,469
With arrest of convulsions, oxygen administration, and other supportive measures, the fetus usually recovers more quickly in utero than following immediate cesarean delivery.

140
00:18:07,469 --> 00:18:15,944
Moreover, the mother is better served if delivery is delayed until the intensity of hypoxia and metabolic acidosis has diminished.

141
00:18:15,944 --> 00:18:27,669
Cardiovascular Toxicity These manifestations generally develop later than those from cerebral toxicity, and they may not develop at all because they are induced by higher serum drug levels.

142
00:18:27,669 --> 00:18:36,694
The exception is bupivacaine, which is associated with the development of neurotoxicity and cardiotoxicity at virtually identical levels .

143
00:18:36,694 --> 00:18:48,107
Fortunately, bupivacaine is mostly used in dilute epidural solutions, and use of 0.75-percent solution is limited to a small dose for spinal analgesia.

144
00:18:48,107 --> 00:18:56,157
Similar to neurotoxicity, cardiovascular toxicity is characterized first by stimulation and then by depression.

145
00:18:56,157 --> 00:19:00,944
Accordingly, hypertension and tachycardia are soon followed by

146
00:19:01,108 --> 00:19:06,533
hypotension, cardiac arrhythmias, and impaired uteroplacental perfusion.

147
00:19:06,483 --> 00:19:10,895
As before, 20-percent intralipids serve as the rescue.

148
00:19:10,895 --> 00:19:17,745
Hypotension is managed initially by turning the woman onto either side to avoid aortocaval compression.

149
00:19:17,745 --> 00:19:22,558
A crystalloid solution is infused rapidly along with vasopressors.

150
00:19:22,558 --> 00:19:33,795
Emergency bedside cesarean delivery is considered if maternal vital signs have not been restored within 4 minutes of cardiac arrest, and the goal is delivery within 5 minutes .

151
00:19:33,795 --> 00:19:42,620
However, as with convulsions, the fetus is likely to recover more quickly in utero once maternal cardiac output is reestablished.

152
00:19:42,620 --> 00:19:45,095
# Pudendal Block.

153
00:19:45,095 --> 00:19:50,383
Pain with vaginal delivery arises from stimuli from the lower genital tract.

154
00:19:50,383 --> 00:20:00,883
These are transmitted primarily through the pudendal nerve, the peripheral branches of which provide sensory innervation to the perineum, anus, vulva, and clitoris .

155
00:20:00,883 --> 00:20:09,145
The pudendal nerve passes beneath the posterior surface of the sacrospinous ligament just as the ligament attaches to the ischial spine.

156
00:20:09,145 --> 00:20:16,070
Sensory nerve fibers of the pudendal nerve are derived from ventral branches of the S2 through S4 nerves.

157
00:20:16,070 --> 00:20:22,695
The pudendal nerve block is a relatively safe, simple method of analgesia for vaginal delivery .

158
00:20:22,695 --> 00:20:33,333
As shown in Figure 25-2, a tubular introducer is used to sheath and guide a 15-cm, 22-gauge needle into position near the pudendal nerve.

159
00:20:33,333 --> 00:20:39,783
The end of the introducer is placed against the vaginal mucosa just beneath the tip of the ischial spine.

160
00:20:39,783 --> 00:20:49,595
The introducer allows 1.0 to 1.5 cm of needle to protrude beyond its tip, and the needle is pushed beyond the introducer tip into the mucosa.

161
00:20:49,595 --> 00:20:57,533
A mucosal wheal is made with 1 mL of 1-percent lidocaine solution or an equivalent dose of another local anesthetic .

162
00:20:57,533 --> 00:21:04,920
To guard against intravascular infusion, aspiration is attempted before this and all subsequent injections.

163
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:12,720
The needle is then advanced until it touches the sacrospinous ligament, which is infiltrated with 3 mL of lidocaine.

164
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:15,933
The procedure is repeated on the other side.

165
00:21:15,933 --> 00:21:18,483
Figure 25-2.

166
00:21:18,483 --> 00:21:21,695
Local infiltration of the pudendal nerve.

167
00:21:21,695 --> 00:21:30,258
Transvaginal technique showing the needle extended beyond the needle guard and passing through the sacrospinous ligament to reach the pudendal nerve.

168
00:21:30,258 --> 00:21:38,958
Within 3 to 4 minutes of injection, the successful pudendal block will allow pinching of the lower vagina and posterior vulva without pain.

169
00:21:38,958 --> 00:21:55,220
If delivery occurs before the pudendal block becomes effective and an episiotomy is indicated, the fourchette, perineum, and adjacent vagina can be infiltrated with 5 to 10 mL of 1-percent lidocaine solution directly at the planned episiotomy site.

170
00:21:55,220 --> 00:21:59,870
The pudendal block usually has become effective by the time of repair.

171
00:21:59,870 --> 00:22:08,170
The maximum recommended total dose of lidocaine is 4.5 mg/kg and not to exceed 300 mg .

172
00:22:08,170 --> 00:22:17,670
For a 50-kg woman, this would equal 225 mg (4.5 mg/kg × 50 kg).

173
00:22:17,670 --> 00:22:29,783
Thus, if 1-percent lidocaine is used, the calculated allowed amount would be: 225 mg ÷ 10 mg/mL = 22.5 mL.

174
00:22:29,783 --> 00:22:35,508
Of note, for any drug solution, 1 percent = 10 mg/mL.

175
00:22:35,508 --> 00:22:43,308
Pudendal block usually does not provide adequate analgesia when delivery requires extensive obstetrical manipulation.

176
00:22:43,308 --> 00:22:54,458
Parenteral or neuraxial anesthesia is usually necessary when complete visualization of the cervix and upper vagina or manual exploration of the uterine cavity is indicated.

177
00:22:54,458 --> 00:22:58,308
Infrequently, complications may follow this block.

178
00:22:58,308 --> 00:23:06,283
As previously described, intravascular injection of a local anesthetic agent may cause serious systemic toxicity.

179
00:23:06,283 --> 00:23:12,608
Hematoma formation from perforation of a blood vessel is most likely when there is a coagulopathy .

180
00:23:12,608 --> 00:23:21,108
The addition of a corticosteroid injected along with the local anesthetic did not decrease the incidence of pudendal neuralgia at 3 months .

181
00:23:21,108 --> 00:23:25,758
Rarely, severe infection may originate at the injection site.

182
00:23:25,758 --> 00:23:33,420
The infection may spread posteriorly to the hip joint, into the gluteal musculature, or into the retropsoas space .

183
00:23:33,420 --> 00:23:36,145
# Paracervical Block.

184
00:23:36,145 --> 00:23:45,458
The cervix, vagina, and uterus are richly supplied by nerves of the uterovaginal plexus (Fig. 2-12, p. 25).

185
00:23:45,458 --> 00:23:50,958
This plexus lies within the connective tissue lateral to the uterosacral ligaments.

186
00:23:50,958 --> 00:23:54,333
Thus, injections are most effective if

187
00:23:54,484 --> 00:24:00,221
placed immediately lateral to the insertion of the uterosacral ligaments into the uterus .

188
00:24:00,171 --> 00:24:12,609
For paracervical blockade, usually 1-percent lidocaine or 3-percent chloroprocaine, 5 to 10 mL, is injected into the cervix laterally at 4 and 8 o’clock positions.

189
00:24:12,609 --> 00:24:18,059
This block usually provides satisfactory pain relief during first-stage labor.

190
00:24:18,059 --> 00:24:24,796
However, because the pudendal nerves are not blocked, additional analgesia is required for delivery.

191
00:24:24,796 --> 00:24:31,059
The block may have to be repeated during labor because these anesthetics are relatively short acting.

192
00:24:31,059 --> 00:24:37,984
Fetal bradycardia is a worrisome complication that develops in approximately 15 percent of these blocks .

193
00:24:37,984 --> 00:24:43,546
Bradycardia usually develops within 10 minutes and may last up to 30 minutes.

194
00:24:43,546 --> 00:24:50,871
Doppler studies have shown an increase in the pulsatility index of the uterine arteries following paracervical blockade .

195
00:24:50,871 --> 00:24:58,459
These observations support the hypothesis of drug-induced arterial vasospasm as a cause of fetal bradycardia .

196
00:24:58,459 --> 00:25:04,709
Thus, paracervical block should not be used in situations of potential fetal compromise.

197
00:25:04,709 --> 00:25:09,584
For all of these reasons, we do not use this block at Parkland Hospital.

198
00:25:09,584 --> 00:25:12,109
Neuraxial Analgesia.

199
00:25:12,109 --> 00:25:15,259
# Neuraxial Regional Blocks.

200
00:25:15,259 --> 00:25:27,146
Epidural, spinal, dural-puncture epidural, or combined spinal-epidural techniques are the most common methods used for relief of pain during labor and/or delivery in the United States.

201
00:25:27,146 --> 00:25:32,759
Nearly 75 percent of mothers receive neuraxial anesthesia for pain relief .

202
00:25:32,759 --> 00:25:44,059
Among first births, 68 percent of women who delivered vaginally received neuraxial pain relief compared with 57 percent of women delivering their second or higher number child.

203
00:25:44,059 --> 00:25:56,396
Antepartum education programs improve the understanding of labor analgesia and increase its use by 30 percent in Hispanic women who traditionally choose labor epidural analgesia less often .

204
00:25:56,396 --> 00:26:11,909
Butwick and associates (2018b) showed women are more likely to receive neuraxial analgesia with higher class of obesity—class I, 72 percent; class II, 73 percent; and class III, 76 percent.

205
00:26:11,909 --> 00:26:17,234
As a brief review, the epidural space lies just outside the dural sac.

206
00:26:17,234 --> 00:26:26,521
The dura mater encases the subarachnoid space, which contains the cauda equina of the spinal cord and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) .

207
00:26:26,521 --> 00:26:33,746
Spinal and intrathecal anesthesia are synonymous, and agents are injected into the subarachnoid space.

208
00:26:33,746 --> 00:26:36,421
Figure 25-3.

209
00:26:36,421 --> 00:26:38,946
Neuraxial analgesia.

210
00:26:38,946 --> 00:26:42,459
A. Combined spinal-epidural analgesia.

211
00:26:42,459 --> 00:26:45,284
B. Epidural analgesia.

212
00:26:45,284 --> 00:26:57,359
Epidural analgesia via a catheter in the epidural space is typically used for relief of labor pain, although it can also be used for anesthesia during operative vaginal and cesarean delivery.

213
00:26:57,359 --> 00:27:06,696
Spinal analgesia is typically given as a single intrathecal injection of a local anesthetic at the time of operative vaginal or cesarean delivery.

214
00:27:06,696 --> 00:27:12,096
Variations of these two basic approaches are described in the subsequent sections.

215
00:27:12,096 --> 00:27:15,634
# Spinal (Subarachnoid) Block.

216
00:27:15,634 --> 00:27:27,896
Introduction of a local anesthetic into the subarachnoid space to effect analgesia offers a short procedural time, rapid blockade onset, and high success rate of inducing analgesia.

217
00:27:27,896 --> 00:27:37,421
The subarachnoid space is smaller during pregnancy likely because of internal vertebral venous plexus engorgement (see Fig. 25-3A).

218
00:27:37,421 --> 00:27:47,621
As a result, compared with nonpregnant women, the same amount of anesthetic agent in the same volume of solution produces a higherlevel blockade in parturients.

219
00:27:47,621 --> 00:27:55,246
Vaginal Delivery Spinal analgesia involving only lower spinal levels can be used for operative vaginal delivery.

220
00:27:55,246 --> 00:28:02,359
The level of analgesia should extend to the T10 dermatome, which corresponds to the level of the umbilicus.

221
00:28:02,359 --> 00:28:07,934
Blockade to this level provides excellent relief from the pain of uterine contractions.

222
00:28:07,934 --> 00:28:15,209
The spinal block provides also excellent analgesia to the lower uterine segment, vagina, and perineum.

223
00:28:15,209 --> 00:28:22,446
Several local anesthetic agents have been used for spinal analgesia (see Table 25-3.

224
00:28:22,446 --> 00:28:30,009
Addition of dextrose to any of these agents creates a hyperbaric solution, which is heavier and denser than CSF.

225
00:28:30,009 --> 00:28:38,584
A sitting position causes a hyperbaric solution to settle caudally, whereas a lateral position will have a greater effect on the dependent side.

226
00:28:38,584 --> 00:28:40,759
Lidocaine given in

227
00:28:40,924 --> 00:28:49,149
a hyperbaric solution produces excellent analgesia and has the advantage of a rapid onset and relatively short duration.

228
00:28:49,099 --> 00:28:54,749
Isobaric bupivacaine provides satisfactory analgesia for more than 1 hour.

229
00:28:54,749 --> 00:29:05,311
Single-dose spinal analgesia is usually administered when the cervix is fully dilated, and all other criteria for safe operative vaginal delivery have been fulfilled .

230
00:29:05,311 --> 00:29:13,174
Preanalgesic IV hydration with 1 L of crystalloid solution will prevent or minimize hypotension in many cases.

231
00:29:13,174 --> 00:29:20,786
Cesarean Delivery A level of sensory blockade extending to the T4 dermatome is desired for cesarean delivery.

232
00:29:20,786 --> 00:29:26,836
A dose of 10 to 12 mg of bupivacaine in a hyperbaric solution is administered.

233
00:29:26,836 --> 00:29:37,086
The addition of 20 to 25 µg of fentanyl improves the quality and prolongs the duration of the spinal block, reduces shivering, and minimizes referred pain.

234
00:29:37,086 --> 00:29:45,261
In addition, 0.1 to 0.2 mg of intrathecal morphine can be added for pain control postoperatively.

235
00:29:45,261 --> 00:29:50,524
With intrathecal morphine, the risk for respiratory depression should be considered.

236
00:29:50,524 --> 00:30:01,999
The Society of Obstetric Anesthesia and Perinatology recommends postoperative respiratory monitoring based on dose, patient risk factors, and perioperative risk factors.

237
00:30:01,999 --> 00:30:15,899
A low-dose regimen, defined as >50 µg and ≤150 µg, in women with no risk factors requires respiratory rate and sedation checks postoperatively for the first 12 hours.

238
00:30:15,899 --> 00:30:27,661
In women with additional risk factors, these safeguards are conducted postoperatively for the first 24 hours, and additional monitoring with pulse oximetry or capnography is considered.

239
00:30:27,661 --> 00:30:35,136
Last, this monitoring method is also recommended in all women receiving doses >150 µg .

240
00:30:35,136 --> 00:30:43,386
Complications Shown in Table 25-4 are some of the more common complications associated with neuraxial analgesia.

241
00:30:43,386 --> 00:30:45,949
Table 25-4.

242
00:30:45,949 --> 00:30:50,124
Complications of Neuraxial Analgesia Hypotension.

243
00:30:50,124 --> 00:30:55,974
This common complication may develop soon after injection of the local anesthetic agent.

244
00:30:55,974 --> 00:31:05,211
It stems from vasodilation caused by sympathetic blockade and is compounded by obstructed venous return due to uterine compression of the vena cava.

245
00:31:05,211 --> 00:31:13,424
In the supine position, even in the absence of maternal hypotension, placental blood flow may still be significantly diminished.

246
00:31:13,424 --> 00:31:22,136
Treatment includes uterine displacement by left lateral patient positioning, rapid IV crystalloid infusion, and IV vasopressors.

247
00:31:22,136 --> 00:31:28,511
One systematic review concluded that vasopressors should be given only in the setting of hypotension .

248
00:31:28,511 --> 00:31:35,286
For treatment, phenylephrine is now the preferred drug to treat hypotension from neuraxial analgesia.

249
00:31:35,286 --> 00:31:40,761
It is a pure alpha agonist and raises blood pressure solely through vasoconstriction.

250
00:31:40,761 --> 00:31:50,749
Maternal bradycardia develops more frequently with phenylephrine than with ephedrine or norepinephrine (Sharkey, 2019; Veeser, 2012).

251
00:31:50,749 --> 00:32:00,036
Although ephedrine was believed to correct hypotension without reducing blood flow, phenylephrine is associated with better umbilical cord blood gases .

252
00:32:00,036 --> 00:32:10,611
A metaanalysis of 36 randomized trials also suggests that both agents have comparable efficacy but that phenylephrine yields a lower rate of fetal acidosis .

253
00:32:10,611 --> 00:32:20,161
For administration, prophylactic infusions of phenylephrine are effective and may require less physician intervention compared with intermittent boluses .

254
00:32:20,161 --> 00:32:35,661
As another potential agent, norepinephrine may improve cardiac output with its beta-adrenergic effects but also is associated with a lower cord arterial pH compared with phenylephrine (Chen, 2021; Mohta, 2019).

255
00:32:35,661 --> 00:32:37,949
High Spinal Blockade.

256
00:32:37,949 --> 00:32:44,886
Most often, complete spinal blockade follows administration of an excessive dose of local anesthetic.

257
00:32:44,886 --> 00:32:52,824
However, it can also develop with inadvertent delivery of a small amount of local anesthetic drugs into the subdural space.

258
00:32:52,824 --> 00:32:59,849
This small potential space lies between the dura mater and arachnoid mater and outside the subarachnoid space.

259
00:32:59,849 --> 00:33:05,674
Because of its limited capacity, even small volumes will reach higher spinal levels.

260
00:33:05,674 --> 00:33:12,149
Hypotension and apnea develop quickly and must be immediately treated to prevent cardiac arrest.

261
00:33:12,149 --> 00:33:22,374
In the undelivered woman: (1) the uterus is immediately displaced laterally to minimize aortocaval compression, (2) effective ventilation is established,

262
00:33:22,540 --> 00:33:30,190
preferably with tracheal intubation, and (3) IV fluids and vasopressors are given to correct hypotension.

263
00:33:30,140 --> 00:33:32,627
Postdural Puncture Headache.

264
00:33:32,627 --> 00:33:38,115
Leakage of CSF from the dural puncture site can lead to postdural puncture headache.

265
00:33:38,115 --> 00:33:47,127
Presumably, when the woman sits or stands, the diminished CSF volume creates traction on pain-sensitive central nervous system structures.

266
00:33:47,127 --> 00:33:55,590
An alternative mechanism is compensatory cerebral vasodilation in response to CSF loss—the Monro-Kellie hypothesis .

267
00:33:55,590 --> 00:34:08,102
The incidence of obstetrical postdural puncture headache in women undergoing spinal analgesia ranges from 0.2 to 1 percent (Maronge, 2012; Sprigge, 2008).

268
00:34:08,102 --> 00:34:16,202
This rate is similar to the overall occurrence of postdural puncture headaches, which approximates 1 headache per 200 blocks.

269
00:34:16,202 --> 00:34:29,277
A study of 567 postpartum hospital encounters showed that 40 percent of presentations for headache are due to neuraxial anesthesia, and these visits most often occur within 7 days of delivery .

270
00:34:29,277 --> 00:34:35,840
Procedural preventive steps include using a small-gauge spinal needle and avoiding multiple punctures.

271
00:34:35,840 --> 00:34:45,940
In a prospective, randomized study of five different spinal needles, Sprotte and Whitacre needles had the lowest risks of associated postdural puncture headaches .

272
00:34:45,940 --> 00:34:57,790
Rates are high with inadvertent dural puncture during epidural analgesia and approximate 0.2 to 1 percent (Introna, 2012; Maronge, 2018).

273
00:34:57,790 --> 00:35:04,852
No strong evidence supports placing a woman flat on her back or providing vigorous hydration to prevent headache .

274
00:35:04,852 --> 00:35:16,490
A prophylactic blood patch performed within 24 hours of dural puncture does not diminish either the incidence of postdural puncture headache or the need for a subsequent therapeutic blood patch .

275
00:35:16,490 --> 00:35:23,977
If a headache develops, administration of caffeine, which is a cerebral vasoconstrictor, has limited efficacy .

276
00:35:23,977 --> 00:35:29,765
With severe headache, an epidural blood patch is most effective and preferred treatment.

277
00:35:29,765 --> 00:35:39,802
Autologous blood is obtained aseptically by venipuncture, and 10 to 20 mL is injected into the epidural space at or below the site of dural puncture.

278
00:35:39,802 --> 00:35:45,265
This site is selected because the blood will spread more readily in the cephalad direction.

279
00:35:45,265 --> 00:35:50,365
Further CSF leakage is halted by either mass effect or clot formation.

280
00:35:50,365 --> 00:35:54,440
Relief is immediate, and complications are uncommon.

281
00:35:54,440 --> 00:36:04,115
If a headache does not have the pathognomonic postural characteristics or persists despite treatment with a repeat blood patch, other diagnoses should be considered.

282
00:36:04,115 --> 00:36:12,865
Chisholm and Campbell (2001) described a case of superior sagittal sinus thrombosis that manifested as a postural headache.

283
00:36:12,865 --> 00:36:18,640
Chan and Paech (2004) reported persistent CSF leak in three women.

284
00:36:18,640 --> 00:36:25,477
Smarkusky and colleagues (2006) described pneumocephalus, which caused immediate cephalgia.

285
00:36:25,477 --> 00:36:38,202
Last, intracranial and intraspinal subarachnoid hematomas have developed after spinal analgesia (Bi, 2021; Maronge, 2018; Moore, 2019).

286
00:36:38,202 --> 00:36:40,077
Convulsions.

287
00:36:40,077 --> 00:36:46,677
Rarely, postdural puncture cephalgia is associated with temporary blindness and convulsions.

288
00:36:46,677 --> 00:36:56,440
Shearer and associates (1995) described eight such cases in 19,000 regional analgesia procedures done at Parkland Hospital.

289
00:36:56,440 --> 00:37:01,440
It is presumed that these also are caused by hypotension from CSF leak.

290
00:37:01,440 --> 00:37:06,652
Immediate treatment of seizures and epidural blood patch were usually effective.

291
00:37:06,652 --> 00:37:08,715
Bladder Dysfunction.

292
00:37:08,715 --> 00:37:17,315
With neuraxial analgesia, bladder sensation is likely to be obtunded and bladder emptying impaired for several hours after delivery.

293
00:37:17,315 --> 00:37:26,102
As a consequence, bladder distention is a frequent postpartum complication, especially if appreciable volumes of IV fluid are given.

294
00:37:26,102 --> 00:37:41,502
Millet (2012) randomized 146 women with neuraxial analgesia to either intermittent or continuous bladder catheterizations and found that the intermittent method was associated with significantly greater rates of bacteriuria.

295
00:37:41,502 --> 00:37:44,427
Arachnoiditis and Meningitis.

296
00:37:44,427 --> 00:37:51,665
Local anesthetics used for neuraxial blocks are required to be free of methylparaben or other preservatives.

297
00:37:51,665 --> 00:38:04,827
This practice, coupled with aseptic technique and disposable equipment, has made meningitis and arachnoiditis rare (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 2010; Maronge, 2018).

298
00:38:04,827 --> 00:38:10,427
Contraindications to Spinal Analgesia Shown in Table 25-5

299
00:38:10,588 --> 00:38:14,863
are absolute contraindications to spinal analgesia.

300
00:38:14,813 --> 00:38:25,688
Obstetrical complications that are associated with maternal hypovolemia and hypotension—for example, severe hemorrhage—are contraindications to spinal blockade.

301
00:38:25,688 --> 00:38:34,163
The additive deleterious cardiovascular effects of spinal blockade plus acute blood loss in nonpregnant patients have been documented .

302
00:38:34,163 --> 00:38:36,900
Table 25-5.

303
00:38:36,900 --> 00:38:48,888
Absolute Contraindications to Spinal Analgesia Severe preeclampsia is another obstetrical complication in which decreased blood pressure can be encountered when neuraxial analgesia is used .

304
00:38:48,888 --> 00:38:53,913
These effects are mitigated by judicial dosing of anesthetic solutions.

305
00:38:53,913 --> 00:39:06,925
Perhaps related, Ulubaşoğlu and colleagues (2018) reported that preeclamptic women undergoing general anesthesia required more antihypertensive medication than those with spinal analgesia.

306
00:39:06,925 --> 00:39:14,125
Disorders of coagulation that result in poor hemostasis also preclude the use of neuraxial analgesia.

307
00:39:14,125 --> 00:39:23,088
Women with a bleeding diathesis such as von Willebrand disease or hemophilias carry an increased risk for subarachnoid hematoma formation .

308
00:39:23,088 --> 00:39:27,813
Rare subdural hematomas may mimic postdural puncture headaches .

309
00:39:27,813 --> 00:39:39,800
However, neuraxial analgesia can be safely performed in these patients following factor replacement and normalization of levels (Choi, 2009; Katz, 2015).

310
00:39:39,800 --> 00:39:46,850
As discussed earlier, these patients should receive consultation with an anesthesiologist prior to labor.

311
00:39:46,850 --> 00:39:54,888
The use of anticoagulation to prevent or treat venous thromboembolism is frequently encountered during the peripartum period.

312
00:39:54,888 --> 00:40:05,788
No randomized studies guide the management of anticoagulation at the time of delivery, but consensus opinion is provided by the Society for Anesthesia and Perinatology .

313
00:40:05,788 --> 00:40:15,975
Briefly, women receiving >10,000 units/d of unfractionated heparin are individually assessed regarding timing and dose of last injection.

314
00:40:15,975 --> 00:40:22,713
Thromboprophylaxis with low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) should have a 12-hour delay.

315
00:40:22,713 --> 00:40:27,650
Therapeutic doses of LMWH merit a 24-hour delay.

316
00:40:27,650 --> 00:40:32,825
This is discussed further in Chapter 55 (p. 985).

317
00:40:32,825 --> 00:40:38,850
Spinal analgesia is also contraindicated if there is infection at the needle entry site.

318
00:40:38,850 --> 00:40:44,800
Other maternal conditions may pose relative contraindications to neuraxial analgesia.

319
00:40:44,800 --> 00:40:57,363
Many women with neurological disorders such as Chiari malformation or pseudotumor cerebri may be candidates for neuraxial analgesia after consultation with an anesthesiologist and neurologist.

320
00:40:57,363 --> 00:41:10,150
Women with severe aortic stenosis or pulmonary hypertension also have a relative contraindication to spinal analgesia due to the deleterious effects of hypotension and diminished preload and afterload .

321
00:41:10,150 --> 00:41:13,063
# Epidural Analgesia.

322
00:41:13,063 --> 00:41:25,300
Relief of labor and childbirth pain, including cesarean delivery, can be accomplished by injection of a local anesthetic agent into the epidural space (see Fig. 25-3B).

323
00:41:25,300 --> 00:41:32,613
This potential space contains areolar tissue, fat, lymphatics, and the internal vertebral venous plexus.

324
00:41:32,613 --> 00:41:38,150
Entry for obstetrical analgesia is usually through a lumbar intervertebral space.

325
00:41:38,150 --> 00:41:47,275
Although only one injection may be given, usually an indwelling catheter is placed for subsequent bolus administration or continuous infusion .

326
00:41:47,275 --> 00:41:52,663
Infusions use a volumetric pump controlled either by the patient or by a caregiver.

327
00:41:52,663 --> 00:42:04,888
Under appropriate physician supervision, labor and delivery nursing personnel with specific training in the management of epidural infusions should be able to adjust dosage and also discontinue infusions.

328
00:42:04,888 --> 00:42:08,850
# Continuous Lumbar Epidural Analgesia.

329
00:42:08,850 --> 00:42:16,988
Complete analgesia for the pain of labor and vaginal delivery necessitates a block from the T10 to the S4 dermatomes.

330
00:42:16,988 --> 00:42:23,425
For cesarean delivery, a block extending from the T4 to the S1 dermatomes is desired.

331
00:42:23,425 --> 00:42:32,525
The effective spread of anesthetic depends upon the catheter tip location and the dose, concentration, and volume of anesthetic agent used.

332
00:42:32,525 --> 00:42:41,000
Individual variations in anatomy or synechiae or septa in the epidural space may preclude a completely satisfactory block.

333
00:42:41,000 --> 00:42:46,250
Last, the catheter tip may migrate from its original location during labor.

334
00:42:46,250 --> 00:42:53,538
Technique One example of the sequential steps and techniques for performance of epidural analgesia is detailed in

335
00:42:53,692 --> 00:42:56,367
Table 25-6.

336
00:42:56,317 --> 00:43:04,742
Before injection of the local anesthetic therapeutic dose, a test dose is given containing small amounts of lidocaine and epinephrine.

337
00:43:04,742 --> 00:43:13,129
The woman is observed for features of toxicity from intravascular injection and for signs of either subarachnoid or subdural injection.

338
00:43:13,129 --> 00:43:19,092
If these are absent, analgesia is initiated with a full dose of the anesthetic alone.

339
00:43:19,092 --> 00:43:26,704
Analgesia is maintained by a continuous infusion or by programmed intermittent boluses delivered by infusion pump .

340
00:43:26,704 --> 00:43:37,454
The addition of a short-acting, lipid-soluble narcotic—fentanyl or sufentanil—lowers the required amount of local anesthetic drug needed to achieve analgesia.

341
00:43:37,454 --> 00:43:40,292
This helps preserve motor function .

342
00:43:40,292 --> 00:43:49,679
One popular regimen is 0.0625- to 0.125-percent bupivacaine with 2-µg/mL fentanyl.

343
00:43:49,679 --> 00:43:52,354
Table 25-6.

344
00:43:52,354 --> 00:44:03,067
Technique for Labor Epidural Analgesia As with spinal blockade, close monitoring, including the level of analgesia, must be performed by trained personnel.

345
00:44:03,067 --> 00:44:11,317
Appropriate resuscitation equipment, drugs, and personnel must be available during administration of epidural analgesia.

346
00:44:11,317 --> 00:44:18,742
Complications As shown in Table 25-4, certain problems are inherent to epidural analgesia.

347
00:44:18,742 --> 00:44:21,029
High Spinal Blockade.

348
00:44:21,029 --> 00:44:29,017
Dural puncture with inadvertent and unrecognized either subarachnoid or subdural injection may cause total spinal blockade.

349
00:44:29,017 --> 00:44:39,617
In a study of more than 18,000 women, the incidence of recognized accidental dural puncture at the time of epidural analgesia was 0.91 percent .

350
00:44:39,617 --> 00:44:45,192
Management of this complication is described earlier (p. 474).

351
00:44:45,192 --> 00:44:47,679
Ineffective Analgesia.

352
00:44:47,679 --> 00:44:56,829
Using currently popular continuous or intermittent epidural infusion regimens, 90 percent of women rate their pain relief as good to excellent .

353
00:44:56,829 --> 00:45:07,017
In a study of almost 2000 parturients, Hess and associates (2001) found that 12 percent complained of three or more episodes of pain or pressure.

354
00:45:07,017 --> 00:45:12,042
Its efficacy was more likely to decrease with rising body mass index .

355
00:45:12,042 --> 00:45:16,704
Inadequate analgesia may result in patient dissatisfaction.

356
00:45:16,704 --> 00:45:19,667
Catheter replacement should be considered.

357
00:45:19,667 --> 00:45:29,217
Moreover, a poorly functioning epidural catheter cannot be used for additional dosing of local anesthetic drugs for intrapartum cesarean delivery.

358
00:45:29,217 --> 00:45:37,629
Such conversion of labor epidural analgesia to cesarean delivery anesthesia has an approximately 20 percent failure rate.

359
00:45:37,629 --> 00:45:42,579
This necessitates placement of a new regional block or general anesthesia .

360
00:45:42,579 --> 00:45:54,067
In a Maternal Fetal Medicine Units (MFMU) Network study, 4 percent of women initially given epidural analgesia required a general anesthetic for cesarean delivery .

361
00:45:54,067 --> 00:46:04,629
Also at times, perineal analgesia for vaginal delivery is difficult to obtain, especially when lower volumes of epidural local anesthetic drugs are used.

362
00:46:04,629 --> 00:46:10,479
With this situation, additional doses can be given, or a pudendal block may be added.

363
00:46:10,479 --> 00:46:12,279
Hypotension.

364
00:46:12,279 --> 00:46:22,442
Sympathetic blockade from epidurally injected analgesic agents may cause peripheral vasodilation with resulting hypotension and diminished cardiac output.

365
00:46:22,442 --> 00:46:35,942
Hypotension is more common—20 percent—in women with an admission pulse pressure <45 mm Hg compared with 6 percent in those whose pulse pressure is >45 mm Hg .

366
00:46:35,942 --> 00:46:42,067
Pulse pressure is calculated by subtracting diastolic from systolic blood pressure measurements.

367
00:46:42,067 --> 00:46:46,729
Obese women also have significantly higher rates of hypotension .

368
00:46:46,729 --> 00:47:00,379
In normal gravidas, hypotension induced by epidural analgesia usually can be prevented by rapid IV infusion of 500 to 1000 mL of crystalloid solution as described for spinal analgesia.

369
00:47:00,379 --> 00:47:04,829
Maintaining a lateral position also minimizes hypotension.

370
00:47:04,829 --> 00:47:13,004
Despite these precautions, hypotension is the most frequent side effect and is severe enough to require treatment in a third of women .

371
00:47:13,004 --> 00:47:15,542
Central Nervous Stimulation.

372
00:47:15,542 --> 00:47:19,579
Convulsions are an uncommon but serious complication.

373
00:47:19,579 --> 00:47:29,492
Described earlier, the immediate management involves infusion of 20-percent intralipids and treatment with lorazepam or diazepam (p. 470).

374
00:47:29,492 --> 00:47:30,979
Fever.

375
00:47:30,979 --> 00:47:35,992
Some women develop intrapartum fever following epidural analgesia.

376
00:47:35,992 --> 00:47:46,804
Many studies are limited by the inability to control for other risk factors, such as labor length, duration of ruptured membranes, and number of vaginal examinations.

377
00:47:46,804 --> 00:47:48,429
With this

378
00:47:48,580 --> 00:47:57,355
in mind, the frequency of intrapartum fever associated with epidural analgesia is 10 to 15 percent above the baseline rate .

379
00:47:57,305 --> 00:48:05,955
The two general theories concerning the etiology of maternal hyperthermia are maternal-fetal infection or dysregulation of body temperature .

380
00:48:05,955 --> 00:48:15,330
A study of placental histopathology in women given epidural analgesia identified intrapartum fever only when there was placental inflammation .

381
00:48:15,330 --> 00:48:18,780
This suggests that fever is due to infection.

382
00:48:18,780 --> 00:48:29,405
Sharma (2014) randomized 400 nulliparas with labor epidural analgesia to receive 2 g cefoxitin prophylactically versus placebo.

383
00:48:29,405 --> 00:48:38,555
It was hypothesized that epiduralrelated fever was due to infection and that prophylactic antimicrobials should significantly reduce the rate of fever.

384
00:48:38,555 --> 00:48:47,505
Approximately equal proportions—40 percent—of women developed fever ≥38°C during labor.

385
00:48:47,505 --> 00:48:55,080
This result suggests that infection is unlikely to cause the fever associated with epidural analgesia during labor.

386
00:48:55,080 --> 00:49:03,017
Whatever the mechanism, women with persistent fever are usually treated with antimicrobials for presumed chorioamnionitis.

387
00:49:03,017 --> 00:49:18,830
The other proposed mechanisms include alteration of the hypothalamic thermoregulatory set point, impaired peripheral thermoreceptor input to the central nervous system with selective blockade of warm stimuli, or imbalance between heat production and heat loss.

388
00:49:18,830 --> 00:49:26,842
Del Arroyo and colleagues (2019) suggested that bupivacaine blocks release of antipyretic interleukins.

389
00:49:26,842 --> 00:49:28,505
Back Pain.

390
00:49:28,505 --> 00:49:34,417
An association between epidural analgesia and back pain has been reported by some.

391
00:49:34,417 --> 00:49:46,742
In a prospective cohort study, Butler and Fuller (1998) reported that back pain after delivery was common with epidural analgesia, however, persistent pain was infrequent.

392
00:49:46,742 --> 00:50:00,280
Based on their systematic review, Lieberman and O’Donoghue (2002) concluded that available data do not support an association between epidural analgesia and development of de novo, long-term backache.

393
00:50:00,280 --> 00:50:02,967
Miscellaneous Complications.

394
00:50:02,967 --> 00:50:08,542
A spinal or epidural hematoma is a rare complication of an epidural catheter .

395
00:50:08,542 --> 00:50:14,042
These are considered neurological emergencies, and treatment is usually surgical.

396
00:50:14,042 --> 00:50:20,155
Epidural abscesses are rare, and the most common pathogen is Staphylococcus aureus .

397
00:50:20,155 --> 00:50:27,080
These may be managed conservatively with IV antibiotics, but surgical intervention may be necessary.

398
00:50:27,080 --> 00:50:32,142
For both conditions, timely neurosurgical consultation is important.

399
00:50:32,142 --> 00:50:36,555
Uncommonly, the plastic epidural catheter is sheared off .

400
00:50:36,555 --> 00:50:40,680
Computed tomography can localize the retained fragment.

401
00:50:40,680 --> 00:50:46,767
For a tip left in the epidural space, surveillance is reasonable in the absence of symptoms.

402
00:50:46,767 --> 00:50:54,592
With neurological changes or with fragments in the subarachnoid space, neurosurgical consultation is recommended .

403
00:50:54,592 --> 00:51:01,267
Last, an association of epidural analgesia and autism spectrum disorder seems unlikely .

404
00:51:01,267 --> 00:51:15,880
Effect on Labor Most studies, including five combined randomized trials from Parkland Hospital shown in Table 25-7, report that epidural analgesia prolongs labor and increases the use of oxytocin stimulation .

405
00:51:15,880 --> 00:51:25,417
Alexander and associates (2002) examined the effects of epidural analgesia on the Friedman (1955) labor curve .

406
00:51:25,417 --> 00:51:34,230
There were 459 nulliparas randomly assigned to receive patient-controlled epidural analgesia or patientcontrolled IV meperidine.

407
00:51:34,230 --> 00:51:41,755
Compared with original Friedman criteria, epidural analgesia prolonged the active phase of labor by 1 hour.

408
00:51:41,755 --> 00:51:49,267
In a cohort study, similar labor durations were observed for both spinal-epidural and epidural analgesia .

409
00:51:49,267 --> 00:51:51,955
Table 25-7.

410
00:51:51,955 --> 00:52:04,405
Selected Labor Events in 2703 Nulliparous Women Randomized to Epidural Analgesia or Intravenous Meperidine Analgesia The effect of labor epidural on the second stage is unclear.

411
00:52:04,405 --> 00:52:13,955
Prolongation of the second stage was particularly true in earlier studies, at a time when higher concentrations of local anesthetic drugs were used.

412
00:52:13,955 --> 00:52:22,055
This association has been attributed to local-anesthetic-induced motor blockade and resultant impaired maternal expulsive efforts.

413
00:52:22,055 --> 00:52:33,442
Chestnut and associates (1999) reported that, with epidural analgesia, operative vaginal delivery occurred more frequently because of prolonged second-stage labor.

414
00:52:33,442 --> 00:52:35,667
In a systematic review,

415
00:52:35,836 --> 00:52:46,298
Anim-Somuah and colleagues (2018) reported that epidural analgesia prolongs the first stage by 32 minutes and the second stage by 15 minutes.

416
00:52:46,248 --> 00:53:01,798
Craig and coworkers (2015) randomly assigned 310 nulliparous women with labor epidural analgesia to receive 0.125-percent bupivacaine plus fentanyl or epidural fentanyl alone during second-stage labor.

417
00:53:01,798 --> 00:53:10,948
Epidural bupivacaine analgesia did cause motor blockade during the second stage, however, the duration of the second stage was not longer.

418
00:53:10,948 --> 00:53:16,023
Neither obstetrical nor neonatal outcomes were different between the two groups.

419
00:53:16,023 --> 00:53:19,698
Patient satisfaction was high with both methods.

420
00:53:19,698 --> 00:53:21,623
Fetal Heart Rate.

421
00:53:21,623 --> 00:53:31,773
Hill and associates (2003) examined the effects of epidural analgesia with 0.25-percent bupivacaine on fetal heart rate patterns.

422
00:53:31,773 --> 00:53:36,473
Compared with IV meperidine, no worrisome effects were identified.

423
00:53:36,473 --> 00:53:43,973
Reduced beat-to-beat variability and fewer accelerations were more common in fetuses whose mothers received meperidine .

424
00:53:43,973 --> 00:53:56,298
Based on their systematic review, Reynolds and coworkers (2002) reported that epidural analgesia was associated with improved neonatal acid-base status compared with meperidine.

425
00:53:56,298 --> 00:53:58,873
Cesarean Delivery Rates.

426
00:53:58,873 --> 00:54:05,736
A contentious issue in the past was whether epidural analgesia increased the risk for cesarean delivery.

427
00:54:05,736 --> 00:54:13,748
Early evidence that it did was from an era when dense motor blockade resulted from high concentrations of local anesthetic agents.

428
00:54:13,748 --> 00:54:21,773
Now however, with the administration of dilute anesthetic solutions, the risk for cesarean delivery is not increased.

429
00:54:21,773 --> 00:54:28,061
Several studies conducted at Parkland Hospital were designed to answer this and related questions.

430
00:54:28,061 --> 00:54:42,186
From 1995 to 2002, a total of 2703 nulliparas at term and in spontaneous labor were enrolled in five trials to evaluate epidural analgesia techniques compared with IV meperidine.

431
00:54:42,186 --> 00:54:51,598
The results from these are summarized in Table 25-8 and show that epidural analgesia does not significantly raise cesarean delivery rates .

432
00:54:51,598 --> 00:55:07,348
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) and the American Society of Anesthesiologists (2016) also conclude that epidural analgesia is not associated with a greater cesarean delivery rate.

433
00:55:07,348 --> 00:55:09,748
Table 25-8.

434
00:55:09,748 --> 00:55:19,461
Cesarean Delivery (CD) Rates in Laboring Women Receiving Epidural Analgesia or Intravenous Meperidine Timing of Epidural Placement.

435
00:55:19,461 --> 00:55:32,923
Earlier studies indicated that epidural placement in early labor was linked to a higher risk of cesarean delivery (Lieberman, 1996; Rogers, 1999; Seyb, 1999).

436
00:55:32,923 --> 00:55:54,661
These observations prompted at least five randomized trials, which showed that the timing of epidural placement has no effect on the risk of cesarean birth, forceps delivery, or fetal malposition (Chestnut, 1994a,b; Luxman, 1998; Ohel, 2006; Wong, 2005, 2009).

437
00:55:54,661 --> 00:56:04,023
Thus, withholding epidural placement until some arbitrary cervical dilation has been attained serves only to deny women maximal labor pain relief.

438
00:56:04,023 --> 00:56:15,198
Safety Among nearly 20,000 women who received epidural analgesia in the MFMU Network study cited earlier, no anesthesia-related maternal deaths were reported .

439
00:56:15,198 --> 00:56:25,523
Ruppen and colleagues (2006) reviewed data from 27 studies involving 1.4 million pregnant women who received epidural analgesia.

440
00:56:25,523 --> 00:56:42,936
Their calculated adverse-event rates were 1:145,000 for deep epidural infection, 1:168,000 for epidural hematoma, and 1:240,000 for persistent neurological injury.

441
00:56:42,936 --> 00:56:53,948
More recently, the Society for Obstetric Anesthesia and Perinatology established the Serious Complication Repository Project (D’Angelo, 2014).

442
00:56:53,948 --> 00:57:00,611
During a 5-year period, more than 228,000 women had neuraxial analgesia.

443
00:57:00,611 --> 00:57:08,861
There were no maternal deaths related to anesthesia, but two each were attributed to cardiac arrest and myocardial infarction.

444
00:57:08,861 --> 00:57:22,986
There were four cases of epidural abscess or meningitis, 58 high-spinal-level neuraxial blocks (1 in 4000), 27 neurological injuries, and 16 respiratory arrests (1 in 10,000).

445
00:57:22,986 --> 00:57:30,373
Contraindications As with spinal analgesia, contraindications are listed in Table 25-5.

446
00:57:30,373 --> 00:57:32,511
Thrombocytopenia.

447
00:57:32,511 --> 00:57:39,498
Although low platelet counts are intuitively worrisome, the level at which epidural bleeding might develop is unknown.

448
00:57:39,498 --> 00:57:43,373
Epidural hematomas are rare, and their incidence

449
00:57:43,540 --> 00:57:55,540
was estimated to be 0 to 0.6 percent in women whose platelet count is <70,000/µL (Goodier, 2015; Lee, 2017).

450
00:57:55,490 --> 00:58:13,190
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) and Society for Obstetric Anesthesia and Perinatalogy have concluded that women with a platelet count of ≥70,000/µL may be candidates for neuraxial analgesia.

451
00:58:13,190 --> 00:58:15,402
Anticoagulation.

452
00:58:15,402 --> 00:58:24,490
Women receiving anticoagulation therapy who are given regional analgesia are at increased risk for spinal cord hematoma and compression .

453
00:58:24,490 --> 00:58:41,327
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) has concluded the following: 1. With prophylactic unfractionated heparin (UFH) in a dose of 5000 units twice daily, regional analgesia is not contraindicated.

454
00:58:41,327 --> 00:58:54,452
2. With an intermediate dose of UFH (7500 to 10,000 units), it is likely low risk to proceed with neuraxial analgesia at a point more than 12 hours after the last dose.

455
00:58:54,452 --> 00:59:13,140
3. With a high dose of UFH (>20,000 units), it is likely low risk to use regional analgesia at a point more than 24 hours after the last dose if the activate partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) is within normal range or the anti-Xa level is undetectable.

456
00:59:13,140 --> 00:59:21,865
4. With thromboprophylaxis with LMWH, needle or catheter placement or removal should be delayed 12 hours or more.

457
00:59:21,865 --> 00:59:30,940
5. With treatment doses of LMWH, placement or removal of a neuraxial catheter should be delayed for 24 hours.

458
00:59:30,940 --> 00:59:33,852
Severe Preeclampsia-Eclampsia.

459
00:59:33,852 --> 00:59:44,165
Concerns with neuraxial analgesia in women with severe preeclampsia include hypotension as well as hypertension from pressor agents given to correct hypotension.

460
00:59:44,165 --> 00:59:49,865
Additionally, pulmonary edema may follow infusion of large volumes of crystalloid.

461
00:59:49,865 --> 00:59:54,602
These risks are outweighed by the disadvantages of general anesthesia .

462
00:59:54,602 --> 00:59:59,915
Of these, tracheal intubation may be difficult because of upper airway edema.

463
00:59:59,915 --> 01:00:09,252
Also, general anesthesia can lead to severe, sudden hypertension that can cause pulmonary or cerebral edema or intracranial hemorrhage.

464
01:00:09,252 --> 01:00:21,065
With improved techniques for infusion of dilute local anesthetics into the epidural space, most have come to favor epidural blockade for labor and delivery in women with severe preeclampsia.

465
01:00:21,065 --> 01:00:34,490
In a study from Parkland Hospital, Lucas and associates (2001) randomly assigned 738 women with hypertension to epidural analgesia or patient-controlled IV analgesia during labor.

466
01:00:34,490 --> 01:00:42,215
A standardized protocol for prehydration, incremental epidural administration, and ephedrine use was employed.

467
01:00:42,215 --> 01:00:48,215
They concluded that labor epidural analgesia was safe in women with hypertensive disorders.

468
01:00:48,215 --> 01:00:58,440
Thrombocytopenia is seen with severe preeclampsia complicated by hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, low platelet count—HELLP syndrome .

469
01:00:58,440 --> 01:01:14,277
Discussed earlier and in general, it seems safe to use neuraxial techniques if the platelet count is ≥70,000/µL (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019b, 2020).

470
01:01:14,277 --> 01:01:21,677
Women with severe preeclampsia can have remarkably diminished intravascular volume compared with normal pregnancy .

471
01:01:21,677 --> 01:01:29,527
Conversely, total body water volume is increased because of the capillary leak caused by endothelial cell activation .

472
01:01:29,527 --> 01:01:37,165
This imbalance is manifested as pathological peripheral edema, proteinuria, ascites, and total lung water.

473
01:01:37,165 --> 01:01:46,602
For all of these reasons, aggressive volume replacement raises the risk for pulmonary edema, especially in the first 72 hours postpartum .

474
01:01:46,602 --> 01:01:56,702
In one study, 3.5 percent of women with severe preeclampsia developed pulmonary edema when preloaded without a protocol limit to the volume infused .

475
01:01:56,702 --> 01:02:06,927
The risk of pulmonary edema can be reduced or obviated with judicious prehydration—usually with 500 to 1000 mL of crystalloid solution.

476
01:02:06,927 --> 01:02:21,140
Specifically, in the study by Lucas and colleagues (2001) cited earlier, no instances of pulmonary edema developed among the women in whom the infused crystalloid preload volume was limited to 500 mL.

477
01:02:21,140 --> 01:02:31,790
Moreover, vasodilation produced by epidural blockade is less abrupt if the analgesia level is achieved slowly with dilute solutions of local anesthetic agents.

478
01:02:31,790 --> 01:02:39,527
With vigorous IV crystalloid therapy, cerebral edema also may develop (Chap. 40, p. 701.

479
01:02:39,527 --> 01:02:43,477
Last, most cases of pharyngolaryngeal edema

480
01:02:43,636 --> 01:02:46,861
are related to aggressive volume therapy .

481
01:02:46,811 --> 01:02:50,236
# Epidural Opioid Analgesia.

482
01:02:50,236 --> 01:03:00,036
Opioids alone usually will not provide adequate analgesia, and they are often used as an adjunct to a local anesthetic agent such as bupivacaine .

483
01:03:00,036 --> 01:03:09,211
This combination lowers the local anesthetic drug concentration needed to achieve analgesia, which in turn creates a less dense motor blockade.

484
01:03:09,211 --> 01:03:14,136
Other benefits are rapid onset of pain relief and a decrease in shivering.

485
01:03:14,136 --> 01:03:18,711
Common side effects include pruritus and urinary retention.

486
01:03:18,711 --> 01:03:26,948
Nalbuphine, a mixed opioid agonist/antagonist, will abolish these symptoms without affecting the analgesic action.

487
01:03:26,948 --> 01:03:37,148
When neuraxial morphine is given for postoperative pain, monitoring for respiratory depression is necessary as discussed earlier (p. 472) .

488
01:03:37,148 --> 01:03:40,786
# Combined Spinal–Epidural Techniques.

489
01:03:40,786 --> 01:03:52,098
The combination of spinal and epidural techniques (CSE) has risen in popularity and may provide rapid and effective analgesia for labor and then for cesarean delivery.

490
01:03:52,098 --> 01:03:56,273
An epidural needle is first placed in the epidural space.

491
01:03:56,273 --> 01:04:07,548
A small-gauge spinal needle is then introduced through the epidural needle into the subarachnoid space—this is called the needle-throughneedle technique (see Fig. 25-3A).

492
01:04:07,548 --> 01:04:15,648
A single bolus of an opioid, a local anesthetic, or sometimes a combination is injected into the subarachnoid space.

493
01:04:15,648 --> 01:04:23,023
A subarachnoid opioid bolus results in the rapid onset of profound pain relief with virtually no motor blockade .

494
01:04:23,023 --> 01:04:29,436
The spinal needle is withdrawn, and an epidural catheter is then placed into the epidural space.

495
01:04:29,436 --> 01:04:34,536
The epidural catheter remains and permits repeated analgesia dosing.

496
01:04:34,536 --> 01:04:47,348
Miro and associates (2008) compared epidural analgesia with CSE analgesia for labor in 6497 women and found the overall outcomes and complications to be similar.

497
01:04:47,348 --> 01:04:55,798
Poma and coworkers (2019) also found comparable analgesia results with epidural or CSE techniques.

498
01:04:55,798 --> 01:05:03,948
They noted that subarachnoid sufentanil was associated with lower uterine contractility, but labor duration was not affected.

499
01:05:03,948 --> 01:05:16,061
A greater incidence of fetal heart rate abnormalities related to uterine hypertonus has been reported with CSE analgesia (Abrão, 2009; Beamon, 2014).

500
01:05:16,061 --> 01:05:24,673
The analgesia failure rate is 11.6 percent for traditional labor epidural and 6.6 percent for CSE .

501
01:05:24,673 --> 01:05:35,823
Although CSE provides a faster onset of labor analgesia, the intrathecal injection of local anesthetic drugs and/or narcotic may cause hypotension and pruritus.

502
01:05:35,823 --> 01:05:39,898
# Continuous Spinal Analgesia During Labor.

503
01:05:39,898 --> 01:05:47,711
The technique of continuous spinal analgesia for relief of labor pain is seldom used because of postdural puncture headaches .

504
01:05:47,711 --> 01:05:59,636
With redesigned needles and catheters, Arkoosh (2008) randomized 429 women to either continuous spinal or conventional epidural analgesia during labor.

505
01:05:59,636 --> 01:06:04,486
Complication rates between these two neuraxial techniques did not differ.

506
01:06:04,486 --> 01:06:08,186
# Dural-Puncture Epidural Analgesia.

507
01:06:08,186 --> 01:06:14,173
Gaining popularity, this technique is similar to CSE, but initial steps differ.

508
01:06:14,173 --> 01:06:24,011
First, a single dural puncture is made using a 25-gauge spinal needle placed through the epidural needle shaft (see Fig. 25-3A).

509
01:06:24,011 --> 01:06:32,848
No anesthetic is injected, but the needle can help confirm correct subarachnoid space placement by the drip of CSF from its distal hub.

510
01:06:32,848 --> 01:06:41,373
After removal of the spinal needle, the epidural catheter is threaded into the epidural space (see Fig. 25-3B).

511
01:06:41,373 --> 01:06:50,123
The dural puncture allows for a small amount of local anesthetic drugs to be translocated from the epidural space to the subarachnoid space.

512
01:06:50,123 --> 01:06:55,661
This aids a faster onset of pain relief than traditional epidural analgesia.

513
01:06:55,661 --> 01:07:07,311
Without the intrathecal injection, dural-puncture epidural analgesia is associated with fewer side effects than CSE analgesia and provides better block quality than an epidural .

514
01:07:07,311 --> 01:07:22,223
A review of five studies with 581 patients showed dural puncture epidural technique was associated with a faster onset of analgesic effects, a reduction in provider-initiated boluses “top-ups,” and better sacral coverage.

515
01:07:22,223 --> 01:07:27,261
The incidence of post-dural puncture headache was not significantly increased .

516
01:07:27,261 --> 01:07:34,761
A study confirmed fewer top-ups and a greater local anesthetic drug sparing effect evaluating the combined use

517
01:07:34,924 --> 01:07:39,099
of this technique and the intermittent bolus drug delivery mode .

518
01:07:39,049 --> 01:07:42,561
# Local Infiltration Analgesia.

519
01:07:42,561 --> 01:07:49,511
A local block is occasionally useful to augment an inadequate or “patchy” regional block that was given emergently.

520
01:07:49,511 --> 01:07:58,974
Rarely, local infiltration may be needed to perform an emergency cesarean delivery to save the life of a fetus in the absence of anesthesia support.

521
01:07:58,974 --> 01:08:01,436
Two methods are described here.

522
01:08:01,436 --> 01:08:11,936
In one technique, the skin is infiltrated along the proposed incision, and the subcutaneous, muscle, and rectus sheath layers are injected as the abdomen is opened.

523
01:08:11,936 --> 01:08:21,736
Injection of large volumes into the fatty layers, which are relatively devoid of nerve supply, is avoided to limit the total dose of local anesthetic needed.

524
01:08:21,736 --> 01:08:31,311
Cooper and coworkers described using 52 mL of 0.5-percent lidocaine with 1:200,000 epinephrine.

525
01:08:31,311 --> 01:08:35,324
This delivered 260 mg of lidocaine.

526
01:08:35,324 --> 01:08:46,174
Related to lidocaine toxicity, higher lidocaine volumes can be used when paired with epinephrine, because vasoconstriction lowers the intravascular transfer of anesthetic.

527
01:08:46,174 --> 01:08:50,086
The maximum dose is 7 mg/kg.

528
01:08:50,086 --> 01:08:58,424
Others have used 1-percent procaine with or without epinephrine (Larsen, 1971; Ranney, 1975).

529
01:08:58,424 --> 01:09:10,761
A second technique involves a field block of the major branches supplying the abdominal wall, to include the 10th, 11th, and 12th intercostal nerves and the ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves .

530
01:09:10,761 --> 01:09:21,024
As shown in Figure 25-4, the former group of nerves is located at a point midway between the costal margin and iliac crest in the midaxillary line.

531
01:09:21,024 --> 01:09:25,299
The latter group is found at the level of the external inguinal ring.

532
01:09:25,299 --> 01:09:29,824
A total of four skin punctures, two on each side, are made.

533
01:09:29,824 --> 01:09:41,086
First, at the intercostal block site, the needle is directed medially, and injection is carried down to the transversalis fascia, avoiding injection of the subcutaneous fat.

534
01:09:41,086 --> 01:09:46,474
Five mL of 0.5-percent lidocaine with epinephrine are injected.

535
01:09:46,474 --> 01:09:55,336
The needle is redirected but not removed, and similar dosing is delivered to sites located at a 45degree angle cephalad and caudad.

536
01:09:55,336 --> 01:09:58,024
The other side is then injected.

537
01:09:58,024 --> 01:10:08,949
Second, at the ilioinguinal and genitofemoral sites, the injection is started at a spot 2 to 3 cm lateral from the pubic tubercle at a 45-degree angle.

538
01:10:08,949 --> 01:10:13,274
Last, the skin overlying the planned incision is injected.

539
01:10:13,274 --> 01:10:15,936
Figure 25-4.

540
01:10:15,936 --> 01:10:19,549
Local anesthetic block for cesarean delivery.

541
01:10:19,549 --> 01:10:29,736
The first injection site is halfway between the costal margin and iliac crest in the midaxillary line to block the 10th, 11th, and 12th intercostal nerves.

542
01:10:29,736 --> 01:10:36,674
A second injection at the external inguinal ring blocks branches of the genitofemoral and ilioinguinal nerves.

543
01:10:36,674 --> 01:10:41,149
Although not shown, both sites are infiltrated bilaterally.

544
01:10:41,149 --> 01:10:45,224
The final site is along the line of proposed skin incision.

545
01:10:45,224 --> 01:10:48,786
# Transversus Abdominis Plane Block.

546
01:10:48,786 --> 01:10:52,474
This block may be used for those with suitable training .

547
01:10:52,474 --> 01:11:07,499
It is usually performed under ultrasound guidance and involves injection of a local anesthetic into the transversus abdominis plane (TAP) between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles (Fig. 2-2, p. 14).

548
01:11:07,499 --> 01:11:14,224
The nerves lying in this plane supply the anterior abdominal wall at the T6 to L1 dermatomes.

549
01:11:14,224 --> 01:11:21,686
Ng and colleagues (2018) concluded that high- versus low-dose local anesthetics performed comparably.

550
01:11:21,686 --> 01:11:27,399
Local infiltration or TAP blocks can be used also for postoperative pain control.

551
01:11:27,399 --> 01:11:32,511
The TAP block in one trial was not superior to local wound infiltration .

552
01:11:32,511 --> 01:11:41,899
Gao and associates (2019) demonstrated that pain relief with the TAP block was comparable with that from patientcontrolled IV analgesia.

553
01:11:41,899 --> 01:11:48,074
Another study found that the TAP block was equivalent to intrathecal morphine for postoperative pain .

554
01:11:48,074 --> 01:11:56,911
Last, Prabhu and coworkers (2018) reported that a liposomal bupivacaine incisional block was not superior to placebo.

555
01:11:56,911 --> 01:11:59,024
General Anesthesia.

556
01:11:59,024 --> 01:12:07,736
Trained personnel and specialized equipment—including fiberoptic intubation—are mandatory for the safe use of general anesthesia.

557
01:12:07,736 --> 01:12:11,424
A common cause of death cited is failed intubation.

558
01:12:11,424 --> 01:12:18,461
The latter occurs in approximately 1 of every 390 general anesthetics administered to pregnant women .

559
01:12:18,461 --> 01:12:24,236
This relatively higher mortality rate suggests that neuraxial analgesia is

560
01:12:24,388 --> 01:12:37,963
the preferred method of pain control and should be used unless contraindicated (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019b; American Society of Anesthesiologists, 2016).

561
01:12:37,913 --> 01:12:51,700
In two reports from the MFMU Network, 93 percent of more than 54,000 cesarean deliveries were performed using neuraxial analgesia (Bloom, 2005; Brookfield, 2013).

562
01:12:51,700 --> 01:13:03,400
Racial and ethnic disparities exist, and a higher incidence of general anesthesia use is seen among non-white women (Butwick, 2014; Lange, 2017).

563
01:13:03,400 --> 01:13:08,475
It is often used for emergent cesarean delivery for acute fetal distress .

564
01:13:08,475 --> 01:13:17,700
In contrast, with obstetrical anesthesiologist subspecialists, the overall likelihood that neuraxial analgesia will be used is higher .

565
01:13:17,700 --> 01:13:20,438
# Patient Preparation.

566
01:13:20,438 --> 01:13:26,975
Before anesthesia induction, several steps should be taken to help minimize complication risks.

567
01:13:26,975 --> 01:13:37,338
First, antacid administration shortly before anesthesia induction has probably decreased mortality rates from general anesthesia more than any other single practice.

568
01:13:37,338 --> 01:13:51,913
The American Society of Anesthesiologists Task Force on Obstetrical Anesthesia (2016) recommends timely administration of a nonparticulate antacid, an H2-receptor antagonist, or metoclopramide.

569
01:13:51,913 --> 01:14:04,850
For many years, we have recommended administration of 30 mL of Bicitra—sodium citrate with citric acid—a few minutes before anesthesia induction by either general or major neuraxial block.

570
01:14:04,850 --> 01:14:12,775
If more than 1 hour has passed after the first dose was given and anesthesia has not yet been induced, a second dose is given.

571
01:14:12,775 --> 01:14:21,200
Lateral uterine displacement also is provided as the uterus may compress the inferior vena cava and aorta when the mother is supine.

572
01:14:21,200 --> 01:14:29,450
With uterine displacement, the duration of general anesthesia has less effect on neonatal condition than if the woman remains supine .

573
01:14:29,450 --> 01:14:42,200
Last, because functional reserve lung capacity is diminished, and oxygen consumption is increased, the pregnant woman becomes hypoxemic more rapidly during periods of apnea than do nonpregnant patients.

574
01:14:42,200 --> 01:14:45,413
Obesity exacerbates this tendency.

575
01:14:45,413 --> 01:14:53,900
To minimize hypoxia between the time of muscle relaxant injection and intubation, nitrogen is replaced by oxygen in the lungs.

576
01:14:53,900 --> 01:15:03,063
This preoxygenation is accomplished by administering 100-percent oxygen via face mask for 2 to 3 minutes before anesthesia induction.

577
01:15:03,063 --> 01:15:11,550
In an emergency, four vital capacity breaths of 100-percent oxygen via a tight breathing circuit will provide similar benefit .

578
01:15:11,550 --> 01:15:21,175
High-flow nasal oxygen was found to be inferior to administration by face mask (Shippam, 2019; Tan, 2019).

579
01:15:21,175 --> 01:15:24,088
# Induction of Anesthesia.

580
01:15:24,088 --> 01:15:30,188
Drugs used to induce general anesthesia include propofol, etomidate, and ketamine.

581
01:15:30,188 --> 01:15:34,413
Propofol has the undesirable side effect of hypotension.

582
01:15:34,413 --> 01:15:38,850
Etomidate is associated with fewer hemodynamic changes.

583
01:15:38,850 --> 01:15:45,350
Ketamine also may be used for induction of general anesthesia in hemodynamically unstable women.

584
01:15:45,350 --> 01:15:47,600
# Intubation.

585
01:15:47,600 --> 01:15:54,213
Immediately after a patient is rendered unconscious, a muscle relaxant is given to aid intubation.

586
01:15:54,213 --> 01:15:59,288
Succinylcholine, a rapid-onset and short-acting agent, is used.

587
01:15:59,288 --> 01:16:08,738
Cricoid pressure—the Sellick maneuver—is applied by a trained assistant to occlude the esophagus from the onset of induction until intubation is completed.

588
01:16:08,738 --> 01:16:14,938
Before the operation begins, proper placement of the endotracheal tube must be confirmed.

589
01:16:14,938 --> 01:16:22,413
As discussed, and although uncommon, failed intubation is a major cause of anesthesia-related maternal mortality.

590
01:16:22,413 --> 01:16:34,375
A history of prior difficult intubation and a careful anatomical assessment of the neck and the maxillofacial, pharyngeal, and laryngeal structures may help predict intubation complications.

591
01:16:34,375 --> 01:16:43,000
Even in cases in which the initial airway assessment was unremarkable, edema may develop intrapartum and present considerable challenges.

592
01:16:43,000 --> 01:16:48,200
Morbid obesity is a major risk factor for failed or difficult intubation.

593
01:16:48,200 --> 01:16:58,975
The American Society of Anesthesiologists Task Force on Obstetric Anesthesia (2016) stresses the importance of appropriate preoperative preparation.

594
01:16:58,975 --> 01:17:09,813
This includes the immediate availability of specialized equipment such as different-shaped laryngoscopes, laryngeal mask airways, and video laryngoscopes, as well as

595
01:17:09,964 --> 01:17:16,489
equipment for transtracheal ventilation and awake oral intubation with a fiberoptic bronchoscope.

596
01:17:16,439 --> 01:17:24,226
Following failed intubation, the woman is ventilated by mask and cricoid pressure is applied to reduce the aspiration risk.

597
01:17:24,226 --> 01:17:29,901
Surgery may proceed with mask ventilation, or the woman may be allowed to awaken.

598
01:17:29,901 --> 01:17:44,814
In those cases in which the woman has been paralyzed and in which ventilation cannot be reestablished by insertion of an oral airway, by laryngeal mask airway, or by use of a fiberoptic laryngoscope, then a life-threatening emergency exists .

599
01:17:44,814 --> 01:17:52,664
To restore ventilation, percutaneous or even open cricothyrotomy is performed, and jet ventilation begun.

600
01:17:52,664 --> 01:17:58,376
Failed intubation drills have been recommended to optimize response to such an emergency.

601
01:17:58,376 --> 01:18:01,439
# Inhalational Anesthetics.

602
01:18:01,439 --> 01:18:09,426
Once the endotracheal tube is secured, a volatile halogenated agent is given to provide amnesia and analgesia.

603
01:18:09,426 --> 01:18:16,214
The most commonly used volatile anesthetics in the United States include desflurane and sevoflurane.

604
01:18:16,214 --> 01:18:22,014
These gases can also produce uterine relaxation when given in high concentrations.

605
01:18:22,014 --> 01:18:36,426
These are used when relaxation is a requisite, such as for internal podalic version of the second twin, breech decomposition, ex utero intrapartum treatment (EXIT) procedures, and replacement of the acutely inverted uterus.

606
01:18:36,426 --> 01:18:38,789
# Extubation.

607
01:18:38,789 --> 01:18:50,764
The endotracheal tube may be safely removed only if the woman is conscious to a degree that enables her to follow commands and is capable of maintaining oxygen saturation with spontaneous respiration.

608
01:18:50,764 --> 01:18:57,064
Consideration should be given to emptying the stomach via a nasogastric tube before extubation.

609
01:18:57,064 --> 01:19:02,789
As induction has now become safer, extubation may be relatively more perilous.

610
01:19:02,789 --> 01:19:18,176
Of 15 anesthesia-related deaths of pregnant women from 1985 to 2003 in Michigan, none occurred during induction, whereas five resulted from hypoventilation or airway obstruction during emergence, extubation, or recovery .

611
01:19:18,176 --> 01:19:20,276
# Fasting.

612
01:19:20,276 --> 01:19:27,564
Data are insufficient regarding fasting times for clear liquids and the risk of pulmonary aspiration during labor.

613
01:19:27,564 --> 01:19:50,714
The American Society of Anesthesiologists Task Force on Obstetrical Anesthesia (2016) and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommend that modest amounts of clear liquids such as water, clear tea, black coffee, carbonated beverages, and pulp-free fruit juices be allowed in uncomplicated laboring women.

614
01:19:50,714 --> 01:20:02,614
As discussed in Chapter 30 (p. 550), enhanced recovery after surgery (ERAS) protocols provide consumption of these same liquids up to 2 hours before scheduled surgery .

615
01:20:02,614 --> 01:20:05,689
Obvious solid foods should be avoided.

616
01:20:05,689 --> 01:20:17,839
A fasting period of 6 to 8 hours, depending on the type of food ingested, is recommended for uncomplicated parturients undergoing elective cesarean delivery or puerperal tubal ligation.

617
01:20:17,839 --> 01:20:26,439
The amount of gastric fluid—2 mL/kg—in nonlaboring women is almost the same as that in nonpregnant women .

618
01:20:26,439 --> 01:20:33,676
However, pregnancy is associated with horizontal shift of the stomach and a reduced esophageal sphincter tone.

619
01:20:33,676 --> 01:20:42,676
Our policy at Parkland Hospital is to allow clear liquids in low-risk uncomplicated laboring women in whom vaginal delivery is anticipated.

620
01:20:42,676 --> 01:20:45,026
# Aspiration.

621
01:20:45,026 --> 01:20:51,714
Massive gastric acidic inhalation may cause pulmonary insufficiency from aspiration pneumonitis .

622
01:20:51,714 --> 01:20:59,939
Such pneumonitis has in the past been the most common cause of anesthetic deaths in obstetrics and therefore deserves special attention.

623
01:20:59,939 --> 01:21:10,601
To minimize this risk, antacids should be given routinely, intubation should be accompanied by cricoid pressure, and regional analgesia should be employed when possible.

624
01:21:10,601 --> 01:21:22,014
Pathophysiology In 1952, Teabeaut demonstrated experimentally that if the pH of aspirated fluid was below 2.5, severe chemical pneumonitis developed.

625
01:21:22,014 --> 01:21:29,751
It was later demonstrated that the gastric fluid pH of nearly half of women tested intrapartum was <2.5 .

626
01:21:29,751 --> 01:21:40,701
The right mainstem bronchus usually offers the simplest pathway for aspirated material to reach the lung parenchyma, and therefore, the right lower lobe is most often involved.

627
01:21:40,701 --> 01:21:45,451
In severe cases, involvement is bilateral and widespread.

628
01:21:45,451 --> 01:21:49,614
The woman who aspirates may develop evidence of respiratory

629
01:21:49,780 --> 01:21:58,405
distress immediately or several hours after aspiration, depending in part on the material aspirated and the severity of the response.

630
01:21:58,355 --> 01:22:04,442
Aspiration of a large amount of solid material causes obvious signs of airway obstruction.

631
01:22:04,442 --> 01:22:11,142
Smaller particles without acidic liquid may lead to patchy atelectasis and later to bronchopneumonia.

632
01:22:11,142 --> 01:22:25,105
When highly acidic liquid is inspired, decreased oxygen saturation along with tachypnea, bronchospasm, rhonchi, rales, atelectasis, cyanosis, tachycardia, and hypotension are likely to develop.

633
01:22:25,105 --> 01:22:35,580
At the injury sites, there is pulmonary capillary leakage and exudation of protein-rich fluid containing numerous erythrocytes into the lung interstitium and alveoli.

634
01:22:35,580 --> 01:22:41,867
This causes decreased pulmonary compliance, shunting of blood, and severe hypoxemia.

635
01:22:41,867 --> 01:22:47,692
Suspicion of aspiration demands close monitoring for evidence of pulmonary damage.

636
01:22:47,692 --> 01:22:55,755
Respiratory rate and oxygen saturation as measured by pulse oximetry are the most sensitive and earliest indicators of injury.

637
01:22:55,755 --> 01:23:00,542
Radiographic changes may not appear immediately and may be variable.

638
01:23:00,542 --> 01:23:06,142
Therefore, chest radiographs alone should not be used to exclude aspiration.

639
01:23:06,142 --> 01:23:13,617
Treatment Inhaled fluid should be immediately and thoroughly wiped from the mouth and removed from the pharynx and trachea by suction.

640
01:23:13,617 --> 01:23:19,292
Saline lavage may further disseminate the acid throughout the lung and is not recommended.

641
01:23:19,292 --> 01:23:26,192
If large particulate matter is inspired, bronchoscopy may be indicated to relieve airway obstruction.

642
01:23:26,192 --> 01:23:34,930
Use of corticosteroid therapy or prophylactic antimicrobial administration are not supported by clinical or experimental evidence .

643
01:23:34,930 --> 01:23:39,367
If infection develops, however, vigorous treatment is given.

644
01:23:39,367 --> 01:23:47,930
Etiological agents have shifted from anaerobic or mixed infections to bacteria causing community- or hospital-acquired pneumonitis .

645
01:23:47,930 --> 01:23:53,292
These infections are discussed in Chapter 54 (p. 962).

646
01:23:53,292 --> 01:24:01,830
If acute respiratory distress syndrome develops, mechanical ventilation with positive end-expiratory pressure may prove lifesaving .

